---
// Sources: Lunaaaalj/Stochastic_RefInit. Final report (reports/final): simulation of planting,
// validation, results and conclusions; docs/presentacion/datos/datos.js, generated by the project
// from the outputs of `refor` with seed 42 (polygon map, minimum budgets, frontier, keys, the
// A. salmiana scenario, exact solver bounds); data/processed (species, competition matrix, the
// current planting order). Extracted by scripts/extract-refinit.mjs.
//
// The method is shown as nine scroll scenes (src/scripts/showcase); the text after them carries
// the results, the planting table and the formulation.
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import TeX from '../components/TeX.astro';
import Scene from '../components/Scene.astro';
import story from '../data/refinit-story.json';

const key = story.keys[0];
const exactFrontier = [14666, 14597, 14496, 14409, 14305, 14233, 14118];
const shortfall = [396, 384, 365, 349, 330, 317, 296];
const fmt = (x: number, d = 0) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: d, maximumFractionDigits: d });
---
<Project
  slug="reforestation"
  abstract="Two decisions for CONAFOR’s reforestation of 30 polygons along the Dominica–Charcas transmission line in San Luis Potosí: how many plants of each of ten native species to buy so that every polygon meets its target with probability at least 95%, and how to arrange them so that neighbouring plants compete as little as possible. At the budget of MXN 12,500 per hectare no polygon is feasible; the minimum is MXN 14,496. The arrangement reduces competition by 14.5% to 16.3% against random, within 4.5% of the optimum."
>
  <script is:inline>
    if (!matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) document.documentElement.classList.add('sx-live');
  </script>
  <div class="r intro">
    <p class="m skip">The method is told in nine figures that play as the page scrolls. <a href="#results-h">Skip to the results</a></p>
  </div>

  <div class="showcase">
    <Scene name="site" fig={1} title="Thirty polygons along a power line" source="Polygons from the project’s digitised map" length={2.4}>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.45">
        <p>CONAFOR is to replant 30 polygons of semi-arid scrub along the Dominica–Charcas transmission line in San Luis Potosí, 183.01 hectares in all.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.45 1">
        <p>Each must reach 658 plants per hectare, of ten native species: four agaves, four prickly pears, mesquite and yucca.</p>
        <p>Two decisions follow: how many plants to buy, and where each one goes.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>

    <Scene name="hectare" fig={2} title="One hectare, 658 places" source="Counts drawn with the report’s means; positions illustrative" length={2.6}>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.3">
        <p>Plants are set out on a staggered grid, 4.19 m apart along a row and 3.63 m between rows: 658 positions to the hectare.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.3 0.62">
        <p>Some are already taken. Established plants are scattered at random, about 129 to the hectare, and each species’ count is Poisson.</p>
        <p class="eqline"><TeX tex={String.raw`E_{is} \sim \operatorname{Poisson}(\lambda_s A_i)`} /></p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.62 1">
        <p>The rest must be planted, and the seedlings must survive their first year: 82% of mesquite do, and 96% of <i>Agave salmiana</i>.</p>
        <p>The purchase is made before either number is known.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>

    <Scene name="nbinom" fig={3} title="Ten thousand futures" source="Computed in this page, seed 42; exact curve from the project" length={3.6}>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.38">
        <p>Take one species in one polygon. <i>Agave salmiana</i> in polygon 1 needs 252 plants; 46 stand already, so 206 new ones must survive.</p>
        <p>Plant them one after another until 206 have lived.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.38 0.66">
        <p>Repeat that ten thousand times. The number of plants it takes changes from one replay to the next.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.66 0.8">
        <p>It follows a negative binomial distribution. The hairline is its exact form; the simulation converges to it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.8 1">
        <p>The purchase is the smallest number that succeeds in 95% of the replays: 220 plants.</p>
        <p class="eqline"><TeX tex={String.raw`\mathbb{P}\left(E_{is} + S_{is}(x_{is}) \ge n_{is}\right) \ge 1 - \alpha`} /></p>
        <p class="small">The same curve is computed for each of the 300 pairs of polygon and species and every candidate target; a multiple-choice knapsack then chooses the cheapest combination of targets.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>

    <Scene name="joint" fig={4} title="Ninety-five per cent, ten times" source="Computed in this page, seed 42" length={2.4}>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.56">
        <p>Every species is bought to the same standard. These are 100 simulated hectares with the report’s purchase of 651 plants; each bar is one species, filled when it meets its target.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.56 1">
        <p>Each species succeeds 95 to 98 times in 100. All ten at once succeed far less often: in 68.4% of the report’s 1,000 simulated hectares.</p>
        <p class="small">The constraint holds species by species. Requiring all ten jointly at 95%, with α = 0.005 for each species, raises the minimum budget to MXN 14,728 per hectare, 1.6% more.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>

    <Scene name="budget" fig={5} title="The budget does not reach" source="Minimum budget of each polygon, from the project’s solver" length={3.4}>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.44">
        <p>Each column is one polygon, as wide as its area and as tall as the least it can cost to meet every target with 95% confidence.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.44 0.58">
        <p class="statement">No polygon is feasible.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.58 0.76">
        <p>Weighted by area, the minimum is MXN 14,496 per hectare, 16% more than the 12,500 available: MXN 365 thousand short over the 183.01 ha.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.76 1">
        <p>Accepting more risk barely helps. From a 1% to a 50% chance of falling short, the minimum falls by 3.7%.</p>
        <p class="small">The plants needed to cover the shortfall, corrected for mortality, already cost more than the budget before any safety margin is added.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>

    <Scene name="springs" fig={6} title="Neighbours compete" source="Random arrangement, seed 42; penalties from the project’s matrix" length={2.4}>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.28">
        <p>Then the arrangement. A crew repeats a tile of 52 positions over the polygon. This one holds the mix of key 1, used in 18 polygons, set out at random.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.28 0.66">
        <p>Each plant has six neighbours, and each pair is a spring. Its weight is the penalty <i>W</i> for those two species side by side: how far their canopies overlap and their roots share soil, more for two of the same species, less for mesquite beside a succulent it shelters.</p>
        <p class="eqline"><TeX tex={String.raw`W_{ab} = \gamma^{\,\mathbf{1}[a=b]}\, T_{ab} N_{ab} - \phi\, F_{ab}`} /></p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.66 1">
        <p>A random tile with this mix totals {key.random.toFixed(2)} on average. The task is the arrangement with the least.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>

    <Scene name="memetic" fig={7} title="A memetic algorithm" source="The project’s algorithm, running in this page, seed 42" length={3.6} controls>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.3">
        <p>Tabu search improves a tile by swapping two plants at a time, always the best swap available, and forbids undoing a swap for the next 5 to 15 moves, so that it cannot circle back.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.3 0.62">
        <p>A genetic algorithm keeps 20 such tiles. Two tournaments choose the parents; the child inherits whole species from one parent, then the other, so it keeps the mix. Tabu search improves it, and it replaces the worst tile if it is better and not a copy.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.62 1">
        <p>The run stops after 100 generations without improvement, at {key.best.toFixed(2)}, 16.2% below random.</p>
        <p class="small">An exact integer program, solved with HiGHS for 300 seconds from the same start, found no better tile and bounds the optimum within {key.gap!.toFixed(1)}%.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>

    <Scene name="practice" fig={8} title="Rows are not enough" source="CONAFOR’s current order, from the project’s data" length={3}>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.3">
        <p>CONAFOR’s crews now plant one sequence of 22 positions, repeated along every row. Started at the same point on each row, every plant stands beside two of its own species: competition 13% above random.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.3 0.64">
        <p>Starting each row five positions further on brings it to 0.85 of random. A sequence for each row leaves half of every plant’s neighbours to the way the rows happen to be started.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.64 1">
        <p>A key in two dimensions fixes every neighbour, along the row and between rows: 0.838 of random.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>

    <Scene name="field" fig={9} title="Six keys for thirty polygons" source="Polygons from the project’s map; keys from its solver" length={3}>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0 0.44">
        <p>Key 1 is laid over the polygon copy after copy, at its true spacing, from one corner.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="beat" data-at="0.44 1">
        <p>Rounded to 52 positions, the 30 purchases reduce to six distinct mixes, and so to six keys. A crew needs one table of four rows for each.</p>
      </div>
    </Scene>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="results-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="results-h">Results</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          With MXN 12,500 per hectare no polygon is feasible. The minimum budget that satisfies every constraint is, on
          average weighted by area, MXN 14,496 per hectare, from 14,361 in polygon 8 to 14,934 in polygon 1, 16% more than
          is available. The two smallest polygons are the most expensive per hectare: the margin needed for a given
          reliability grows roughly with the square root of the target, so it weighs more where the target is small.
        </p>
        <p>
          Without the budget constraint the purchase is 108,680 plants for MXN 2.65 million, 593.8 per hectare. With the
          128.8 per hectare already established, planting density is 722.6 per hectare, 9.8% over target: the overplanting
          that absorbs first-year mortality at the required reliability. The composition moves to the edge of the ±10%
          band in the direction of cost per surviving plant: the four prickly pears rise, mesquite, <i>A. scabra</i> and
          yucca fall. That band, which the plan treats as a field tolerance, is the model’s main lever on cost.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>Logistics weigh more than risk: with a 10% logistics overhead instead of 5%, the minimum rises to MXN 15,186 per hectare.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <table class="data">
          <caption class="vh">Minimum area-weighted budget by risk level, by simulation and exactly, and the shortfall against MXN 12,500 per hectare.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Risk α</th><th scope="col" class="n">Monte Carlo, MXN/ha</th><th scope="col" class="n">Exact, MXN/ha</th><th scope="col" class="n">Shortfall, MXN thousands</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {story.frontier.map(([a, b], i) => (
              <tr class={a === 0.05 ? 'hl' : undefined}><th scope="row">{a.toFixed(2)}</th><td class="n">{fmt(Math.round(b))}</td><td class="n">{fmt(exactFrontier[i])}</td><td class="n">{shortfall[i]}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">From the final report. The exact column evaluates the chance constraint with the binomial tail; Monte Carlo error is of the order of MXN 1 per hectare.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          The memetic algorithm reduces competition by 14.5% to 16.3% against a random arrangement of the same composition,
          with a standard deviation over 30 runs of at most 0.006. In every key only one or two of the 156 neighbouring
          pairs are of the same species, always <i>A. salmiana</i>, which fills 27–29% of positions. Mesquite, with the
          largest same-species penalty, never stands beside its own species: it separates the abundant succulents and
          shelters them.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>The keys are robust to the two assumed parameters of the matrix, but an error of ±30% in the species’ traits leaves them on average 6.6% above the keys the correct traits would give.</p></aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <table class="data">
          <caption class="vh">Planting keys of 52 positions: polygons sharing each, competition of a random arrangement and of the memetic algorithm's best, the exact program's lower bound and the largest possible gap.</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col" class="n">Polygons</th><th scope="col" class="n">Random</th><th scope="col" class="n">Memetic</th><th scope="col" class="n">Lower bound</th><th scope="col" class="n">Gap</th><th scope="col" class="n">Reduction</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {story.keys.map((k) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{k.id}</th><td class="n">{k.polygons.length}</td><td class="n">{k.random.toFixed(2)}</td><td class="n">{k.best.toFixed(2)}</td><td class="n">{k.bound!.toFixed(2)}</td><td class="n">{k.gap!.toFixed(1)}%</td><td class="n">{((1 - k.coef) * 100).toFixed(1)}%</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">From the final report. The lower bound is HiGHS’s after 300 s; it reaches its final value within the first 4 s, which points to a weak linear relaxation rather than a poor solution.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="order-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="order-h">Planting order of key 1</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <div class="order-wrap">
          <table class="data order">
            <caption class="vh">Planting order of key 1: four rows of 13 species codes.</caption>
            <thead><tr><th scope="col">Row</th>{key.grid[0].map((_, i) => <th scope="col" class="c">{i + 1}</th>)}</tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {key.grid.map((row, r) => (
                <tr><th scope="row">{r + 1}</th>{row.map((s) => <td class={`c g${story.genus[s]}`}>{story.codes[s]}</td>)}</tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note">Each row of ground follows its line and starts it again at the end: 4.19 m between plants, 3.63 m between rows, each row starting 2.10 m further on. After row 4 comes row 1. It serves the 18 polygons that share key 1.</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="note"><p>This table is what the crew carries: it fixes every plant’s neighbours, along the row and between rows.</p></aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="buy-h">
    <div class="r"><h2 class="m" id="buy-h">Formulation</h2></div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          For polygon <TeX tex="i" /> of area <TeX tex="A_i" /> and species <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex="s" />,</span> the plants already
          present are <TeX tex={String.raw`E_{is}`} /> and the survivors of <TeX tex="x" /> new plants are <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`S_{is}(x) \sim \operatorname{Bin}(x, q_s)`} />.</span>{' '}
          The decisions are the target <TeX tex={String.raw`n_{is}`} /> and the purchase <span class="nowrap"><TeX tex={String.raw`x_{is}`} />,</span> both integer.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Purchasing</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\min_{n,\,x}\; (1+\theta)\sum_s c_s x_{is} + \sum_s c^R_s\, \mathbb{E}\!\left[D_{is}\right]`} /><span class="eqn">(1)</span></div>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\sum_s n_{is} = 658\,A_i, \qquad \lvert n_{is} - n^0_s\, A_i \rvert \le \delta\, n^0_s\, A_i`} /><span class="eqn">(2)</span></div>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`(1+\theta)\sum_s c_s x_{is} \le B\,A_i`} /><span class="eqn">(3)</span></div>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\mathbb{P}\!\left(E_{is} + S_{is}(x_{is}) \ge n_{is}\right) \ge 1 - \alpha`} /><span class="eqn">(4)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>The cost of the plants with logistics, plus the expected cost of replacing a shortfall <TeX tex={String.raw`D_{is}`} />; a fixed density, each species within 10% of the plan; the budget; the chance constraint, with <TeX tex={String.raw`\alpha = 0.05`} />.</p>
        <p>The smallest purchase that satisfies (4) is the <TeX tex={String.raw`1-\alpha`} /> quantile of the plants needed for <TeX tex={String.raw`n_{is}-E_{is}`} /> survivors, a negative binomial (Fig. 3).</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m">
        <p class="eq-label">Arrangement</p>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`\min_{a}\; \sum_{\{u,v\} \in \mathcal{E}} W_{a_u a_v}, \qquad \lvert \{u : a_u = s\} \rvert = m_s`} /><span class="eqn">(5)</span></div>
        <div class="eq"><TeX display tex={String.raw`W_{ab} = \gamma^{\,\mathbf{1}[a=b]}\; T_{ab}\, N_{ab} - \phi\, F_{ab}`} /><span class="eqn">(6)</span></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="note">
        <p>Over the 156 neighbouring pairs of the periodic tile, with each species’ count fixed. <TeX tex={String.raw`T_{ab}`} />: canopy overlap; <TeX tex={String.raw`N_{ab}`} />: shared root depth; <TeX tex={String.raw`\gamma = 1.5`} />; <TeX tex={String.raw`\phi = 0.3`} /> credits mesquite as a nurse plant.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
    <div class="r">
      <div class="m prose">
        <p>
          Plant-by-plant coordinates are not in the data, so <TeX tex="W" /> is built from species traits rather than
          estimated from field observations. Survival comes from a single year of monitoring and is assumed independent
          between plants; a dry year would affect them all at once. Everything is written in C++20 and is reproducible:
          the same seed gives the same result on any platform and with any number of threads.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Project>

<style>
  .intro { margin-top: 28px; }
  .skip { margin: 0; font: 400 14px/1.45 var(--sans); color: var(--graphite); }
  .showcase { margin-top: 48px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .table-note { margin: 8px 0 0; font: 400 14px/1.45 var(--sans); color: var(--graphite); }
  .data .hl th, .data .hl td { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink); }
  .order-wrap { overflow-x: auto; }
  .order { font-family: var(--cond); }
  .order th.c, .order td.c { text-align: center; padding: 6px 4px; }
  .order thead th.c { color: var(--graphite); font-weight: 400; }
  .order td.c { box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--g); }
  .order td.g0 { --g: var(--genus-1); } .order td.g1 { --g: var(--genus-2); }
  .order td.g2 { --g: var(--genus-3); } .order td.g3 { --g: var(--genus-4); }
</style>

<script>
  import { mountScenes } from '../scripts/showcase/engine';
  import site from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/site';
  import hectare from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/hectare';
  import nbinom from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/nbinom';
  import joint from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/joint';
  import budget from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/budget';
  import springs from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/springs';
  import memetic from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/memetic';
  import practice from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/practice';
  import field from '../scripts/showcase/scenes/field';
  mountScenes({ site, hectare, nbinom, joint, budget, springs, memetic, practice, field });
</script>
